=== SURVEY ===
Scope of this prompt: write a durable architectural decision in the log explaining
WHY phase 39 will refactor change-feed-vs-state separation, before any code is
written in subsequent prompts.

Affected packages and symbols (will be edited in later prompts 01..99, NOT in this
prompt):
  - internal/database/signal_log_reader.go
      KEEP   : SignalTrace, BrickVoltageHistory, DriveAggregates, RegenEnergy,
               ChargeAggregates, LatestTimestamp
      DELETE : SnapshotAt, SignalAt, SignalTracePivot, SignalTracePivotFlat,
               SnapshotBetween (broken state-derivation primitives that return
               raw change-feed rows with null gaps wherever a signal has not
               re-emitted recently)
  - internal/database/signal_history_writer.go
      DELETE : SnapshotAt, SignalAt, SnapshotBetween, GetLatestPerSignal
               (duplicates of the broken state-derivation primitives on the
               writer type)
  - internal/signal/state_reader.go            (NEW - interface)
      ADD    : signal.StateReader interface with State(ctx, vehicleID, at) ,
               SignalAt(ctx, vehicleID, signalName, at), and
               Timeline(ctx, vehicleID, signalName, from, to) - all forward-fold
               by construction so unchanged signals carry forward.
  - internal/signal/state_reader_log.go        (NEW - implementation)
      ADD    : default *database.DB-backed implementation of signal.StateReader
               that owns its own SQL against signal_log; performs forward-fill
               from a prior seed per Tesla Fleet Telemetry's emit-on-change
               semantics.
  - 25+ handler files in internal/api/ (media-player, tire-pressure, climate,
    security, charging, drives, energy, dashboard, etc.) + cmd/teslasync/main.go
    warmup + internal/api/chatbot_handler.go raw-positions reader: migrate
    state reads off the deleted database primitives onto signal.StateReader,
    one site per prompt for atomicity (prompts 10..36).
  - .github/ARCHITECTURE.md (or ADR-002 file): codify "signal_log is a change
    feed; state queries MUST go through signal.StateReader" (prompt 01).
  - Layering-audit Go tests (prompts 41 + 42) prevent regression by failing CI
    if anyone (a) re-introduces a deleted state-read method on
    database.SignalLogReader / database.SignalHistoryWriter, or (b) reads
    "current state" via raw `FROM positions/security_events/etc.
    ORDER BY ts DESC LIMIT 1` SQL on snapshot tables.
  - Final gate at prompt 99 verifies the whole phase end-to-end.

Authoritative spec citation:
  Tesla Fleet Telemetry "System Behavior" -
  https://developer.tesla.com/docs/fleet-api/fleet-telemetry#system-behavior
  Worked example at 2300ms: "Nothing is pushed to the event collector since
  speed has not changed." Unchanged signals are NEVER re-sent. Therefore
  signal_log is a change feed, not a state table.

Symptoms today (proof the bug is real and pervasive, not local):
  - /media-player Playback History: 369 mostly-empty rows; "Spotify" source
    vanishes from rows where media_now_playing_source did not re-emit.
  - tire-pressure history: pressures show '--' on rows where TPMS values
    were unchanged.
  - climate snapshots: HVAC mode and target temp blank when unchanged.
  - security current-state: lock/sentry state nulls when unchanged.
  - charging telemetry: charge rate/limit blank when unchanged.
  All of these handlers call the broken database state-derivation primitives
  (SnapshotAt / SignalAt / SignalTracePivot / SnapshotBetween) which return
  raw signal_log change-feed rows with no forward-fill.

=== REASONING ===

The fix is ARCHITECTURAL, not local. Patches are explicitly REJECTED.

Why patches are rejected:
A point patch (e.g. adding a new SignalTraceForwardFill alongside the broken
SignalTracePivot) leaves the broken primitive available for new code to call.
The next handler author sees both options in autocomplete on
database.SignalLogReader / database.SignalHistoryWriter and picks the wrong
one - or a future refactor in any of the 25+ existing call sites silently
re-introduces the bug. We have already paid this cost once: the duplicate
broken methods on database.SignalHistoryWriter exist precisely because the
broken primitive on database.SignalLogReader looked legitimate and was
copied. Adding more methods to the same broken surface compounds the
mistake. The only durable fix is to move the responsibility off the
database package entirely so the wrong choice is not even available.

Why signal_log is a change feed, not a state table:
Per Tesla Fleet Telemetry System Behavior
(https://developer.tesla.com/docs/fleet-api/fleet-telemetry#system-behavior),
a field is emitted only when interval_seconds elapsed AND the value changed
since the last emit. The spec's worked example at 2300ms states explicitly:
"Nothing is pushed to the event collector since speed has not changed."
Unchanged signals are never re-sent. signal_log faithfully records that
emit stream. Reading "state at time T" therefore requires forward-folding
from a prior seed (the most recent emit at-or-before T for each signal),
not selecting raw rows around T. Any reader that returns raw rows around T
WILL produce the null-gap pathology we are seeing across 25+ pages.

Where state derivation belongs:
State derivation is a SEMANTIC operation on top of the change feed - it
encodes the emit-on-change rule itself - so it belongs in the
internal/signal/ package, not in internal/database/. internal/database/
is the persistence layer; it owns SQL, transactions, indexes, retention,
and aggregation rollups. internal/signal/ owns the signal model and the
in-process L1 SignalStore today. Adding signal.StateReader there extends
the existing pattern (signal package owns "what a signal means"), keeps
the database package free of semantics it cannot test in isolation, and
gives every caller exactly one obvious import path: signal.StateReader.

Why the Go compiler will enforce correctness:
Once SnapshotAt / SignalAt / SignalTracePivot / SignalTracePivotFlat /
SnapshotBetween are DELETED from database.SignalLogReader (prompts 37-39),
and SnapshotAt / SignalAt / SnapshotBetween / GetLatestPerSignal are
DELETED from database.SignalHistoryWriter (prompt 40), no handler can
call them. The Go compiler will fail the build for any file that still
references the removed symbols. There is no "deprecated but works"
intermediate state where a tired reviewer can miss a reintroduction. The
removal is enforced mechanically by the toolchain. This is the strongest
possible regression guarantee available without runtime sandboxing, and
it is the entire reason the fix must DELETE the methods, not deprecate
them.

Why ADR-002 is required:
The compiler enforces that the deleted methods cannot be called - it does
NOT enforce that no one adds a NEW method named SnapshotAt to
database.SignalLogReader six months from now, nor that no one writes raw
"SELECT ... FROM signal_log ... ORDER BY ts DESC LIMIT 1" in a new
handler. ADR-002 (prompt 01) records the rule durably in
.github/ARCHITECTURE.md so reviewers can cite it, and the layering-audit
Go tests (prompts 41-42) make the rule machine-checkable in CI:
  - prompt 41: AST/regex audit that fails if any *.go file outside
    internal/database/signal_log_reader.go and the test itself declares
    a method named SnapshotAt / SignalAt / SignalTracePivot /
    SignalTracePivotFlat / SnapshotBetween / GetLatestPerSignal on a
    database type.
  - prompt 42: AST/regex audit that fails if any handler under
    internal/api/ contains a "current state" SQL pattern such as
    `FROM positions ... ORDER BY ts DESC LIMIT 1`,
    `FROM security_events ... ORDER BY ts DESC LIMIT 1`, etc., on
    snapshot tables. Phase 38 final gate already cleared the
    chatbot_handler_chat.go raw-positions read it had previously
    surfaced (commit 2b8ff5123 "fix(api): route chatbot battery query
    through layered live-state contract"); the audit in prompt 42 will
    then guard the floor against re-introduction.

Migration scope and atomicity:
25+ handler files in internal/api/ plus cmd/teslasync/main.go warmup plus
internal/api/chatbot_handler.go raw-positions reader will be migrated
across prompts 10-36, ONE call site per prompt, so each prompt has a
single owner, a single gate, and an obvious revert. Prompts 37-40 then
DELETE the broken primitives from the database package types in two
batches:
  - prompts 37-39: SnapshotAt, SignalAt, SignalTracePivot,
    SignalTracePivotFlat, SnapshotBetween from database.SignalLogReader
  - prompt 40: SnapshotAt, SignalAt, SnapshotBetween, GetLatestPerSignal
    from database.SignalHistoryWriter
Prompts 41-42 add the layering audits. Prompt 99 is the final gate that
re-runs everything end-to-end and verifies the phase is green.

